            <h2 class="comments-header">2 Comments</h2>
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-18" class="comment">
                <p><strong>Chris Hawes (dadoffive), <a href="https://thebaseballchronicle.com/personal_stories/the_hold_of_the_table#comment-18" title="Comment permalink">Apr  8, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>That was tremendous! Thank you I honestly get teared up and goose bumps reading stuff like this.I will never in my entire life and I mean ever forget the spring afternoon in 1975 when the UPS truck rolled up to my house and the driver got out with Stat-o-matic under his arm.I am gonna be 47 in May and have been a tabletop baseball game geek for 35 years! For the last 6 it's been Replay Baseball which in my opinion is the greatest tabletop baseball game EVER!! And although I wait anxiously (just like 1975) for the computer pc version to be released i will never stop playing on the kitchen table too.Only now my sons join me (14 &amp; 16) which is beyond description as far as goose bumps go! So thanks for taking one deep for us geeks,we need not be ashamed anymore. And by the way,my wife has been known to roll her eyes from time to time when she see the charts spread out on the table and hears the dice rollin'</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-19" class="comment">
                <p><strong>Dennis Feller, <a href="https://thebaseballchronicle.com/personal_stories/the_hold_of_the_table#comment-19" title="Comment permalink">Apr  8, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>Great article that brings back melancholy memories. I too waited for the Special Delivery game of my dreams only it was in 1959 or so and the game was BLM (Big League Manager baseball). I am 63 and can't wait till I retire to pick up playing the game again.I played sporadically into my late 20's but when the kid's came along time for the game dissipated. I still own several seasons of player cards (1975 &amp; 1998) as well as six or seven classic old-timer's teams (ex: 1961 Yankees &amp; Dodgers, 1934 Cardinals, 1954 Giants). Although I am  happily married to the same woman for 40 years I still reminisce about the days when I went to my "special place" and immersed myself in the fantasy world of table-top baseball for hours on end.
I also own a table-top football game called "Paydirt" originally put out by Sports Illustrated and look forward to playing that during the winter months of my retirement.
Glad to have found "the cchronicle" and look forward to reading more memory and thought- provoking articles.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    


    
    


